WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.209 --> 00:00:04.240
Hello, Jubilee and all the Goodhumans out there.
- Hi, Jubilee.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:12.900
[Everyone talking over each other in different languages; they're introducing themselves]

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:36.000
At the moment, what's really scary to me is the fact that no one really knows how to handle the whole situation

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.460
Cause, my job got shut down for two weeks

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.080
My mum lost her job

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:40.880
Dominic's job got cut

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.000
And we lived paycheck to paycheck

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.320
I'm almost afraid of what I don't know

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:45.820
What's coming in the future

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.520
[Everyone talking]

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.000
Uncertainty, Uncertainty, Uncertainty

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
This is the longest I've really had to spend with my thoughts

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.800
I have recognised some self destructive behaviour.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.220
I myself struggle with depression

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:07.000
And I'm home and there's no people around me. I really don't have a good way to distract myself.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Our family life hasn't been...well melding together. And...*sighs* I'm emotionally breaking down

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.300
Where I work is insane.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:27.180
At 6:30am there's already people waiting outside. It's ridiculous.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.600
We are literally risking our lives for this.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.780
We saw a disturbing rise in hate-filled attacks.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:45.820
Right now I'm most afraid about my own health because I have underlying medical conditions.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.560
I have a chronic auto-immune illness

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.300
I also have Type 1 diabetes

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.520
I'm missing a part of my lung so if I catch COVID-19 I could possibily die.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.840
My parents, my dad, my sister, I have a newborn baby brother

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:01.180
My mom is a nurse. My dad is still working and his company won't give him a leave.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.900
My dad has a history with cancer. My mom is immunocompromised.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:18.000
What I am most afraid of right now is my loved ones getting sick and particularly my boyfriend getting sick because he has a lot of health issues that really put him at a higher risk.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:38.500
Because currently, my mom is still working. I wish she could stay at home and not have to work so hard because I can sacrifice not graduating. But I can't sacrifice my family

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:53.120
Right before I started recording this video my grandpa was shipped off to hospital because he had a fever, shivering...couldn't control his body.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:01.300
It's not looking too good and I'm honestly really scared right now.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:13.760
I called my dad about 5 days ago and apparently he had been sick for quite some time and he didn't tell me. When he got tested it turned out he was positive for COVID-19.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:23.240
It's so different when you can literally hear someone's shortness of breath through the phone...

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.820
[Sirens]

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.000
I have a lot of questions in my head

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:35.000
[Starts crying]

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:46.060
You don't get to just stay with them to see them get better or you don't get to say goodbye

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.000
You just see a number..

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:03.000
You don't get to celebrate who they were. You don't get to show how much you loved them.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:42.000
There's a white blanket hanging outside. It's a sign of reuniting with the caregivers. As you can see there's people out here on the window.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.760
So everyday here at 8. Everynight at 10:00

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.760
Around 6pm. At 8:00

00:04:55.640 --> 00:05:01.680
The medical staff, doctors, nurses, scientists and researchers. The people who are endangering their own lives

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:06.420
Truck drivers and the grocery store workers. They are the unsung heros

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:10.840
(cheering and clapping)

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:15.140
"You are my hero"

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.980
San Diego Girl Scouts answered the call by sewing homemade masks

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:24.220
So many people who don't have anything to do with eachother have now something to share

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:28.340
That's I think has kind of a strange beauty to it.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:38.200
The fact that how much we still grasp to life even if death's just walking the streets that we knew so well

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:50.100
The fact that we still want to love, to smile, to see people live, to enjoy life even if we're in our houses

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.440
"Here to welcome our first COVID graduate.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:57.460
[Applause]

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.360
[Singing]

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.040
The small acts of resilience

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.220
or the unity that I feel with my fellow citizens.

